=base:summary.cmark
Six ways to embed tramaj in a host, depending on your language: import the library, or work from tramaj-cli's JSON output.

=base:main-content.cmark

# Integrating tramaj into a host

Every integration has the same two moving parts, regardless of language:

1. **Evaluate** — parse a template and run it against a JSON context to get
   a `Node`: the normative, host-agnostic document tree
   ([`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html)).
2. **Fold** — walk that `Node` and turn it into whatever your host actually
   renders: real DOM, a UI framework's virtual tree, another markup
   language, a config file.

Nothing about `Node` or the evaluator knows about HTML, a browser, or any
particular UI framework (see [Properties](/properties.html)) — the fold is
always something *you* write for your host, even where this repository
already ships one for you. Which of the six paths below applies depends
only on what language your host is written in.

The implementations and how they relate: one specification and corpus, six
independently written ports, and the folds and CLIs layered on top of two of
them.

![The specification and corpus feed five ports: tramaj (PureScript) with tramaj-halogen and tramaj-cli, tramaj-hs, tramaj-rs with tramaj-cli-rs, tramaj-js with tramaj-react, tramaj-py, and tramaj-go](/gen/images/implementations.dot.png)

<table>
<tr><th>Host language</th><th>Get the <code>Node</code></th><th>Write the fold</th></tr>
<tr><td>PureScript</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-purs</code> directly</td><td>reuse <code>tramaj-halogen</code>, or write your own</td></tr>
<tr><td>Haskell</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-hs</code> directly</td><td>write your own — none shipped yet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rust</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-rs</code> directly, or shell out to <code>tramaj-cli-rs</code></td><td>write your own — none shipped yet</td></tr>
<tr><td>JavaScript / TypeScript</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-js</code> directly, or shell out to <code>tramaj-cli</code></td><td>reuse <code>tramaj-react</code>, or write your own</td></tr>
<tr><td>Python</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-py</code> directly, or shell out to <code>python -m tramaj</code></td><td>write your own — none shipped yet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go</td><td>depend on <code>tramaj-go</code> directly, or shell out to its <code>tramaj-go</code> CLI</td><td>write your own — none shipped yet</td></tr>
<tr><td>anything else</td><td>shell out to <code>tramaj-cli</code> or <code>tramaj-cli-rs</code>, or reimplement the evaluator</td><td>write your own, over the JSON</td></tr>
</table>

## Numbers at the boundary

The language has two number types, an integer and a float, and a JSON number
is typed by its text: `3` is an integer, `3.0` and `3e0` are floats
([reference](/reference-language.html), section 3). This matters to a host
in three places.

- **Reading the context.** The host's JSON parser must keep the difference
  between `3` and `3.0`, and must not round a large integer. JavaScript's
  `JSON.parse` and Haskell's `aeson` `Value` do neither, which is why
  `tramaj-purs` and `tramaj-hs` each ship a `Tramaj.Json` module with a
  reader (`jsonParser`) and a writer (`stringify`).
  `tramaj-rs` has the same in `tramaj_rs::json` (`parse`, `stringify`).
  `serde_json` keeps `3` and `3.0` apart, but rounds an integer beyond 64
  bits and reads `-0` as a float.
- **Reading the result.** The output keeps the type: an integer is written
  as digits and a float always with a fraction or an exponent
  ([`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html), *Numbers*). A fold that
  renders a scalar child should write it the same way, as `str` does:
  `tramaj-halogen` renders the float `1.0` as `1.0`.
- **Choosing whether templates may compute.** The nine arithmetic builtins
  are an optional profile, chosen by the host for each evaluation and off by
  default. In symbolic mode with the profile on, a result may hold a *term*,
  `{"$term": "sum", "arguments": [...]}`: arithmetic over a symbol, left for
  the host to evaluate ([`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html),
  section 1.9). A host that has not turned the profile on never receives one.

<table>
<tr><th>Implementation</th><th>Two number types</th><th>Arithmetic profile</th><th>Integer range</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-purs</code>, <code>tramaj-halogen</code>, <code>tramaj-cli</code>, playground</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td><code>-(2^53 - 1)</code> to <code>2^53 - 1</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>signed 64-bit</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-rs</code>, <code>tramaj-cli-rs</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>signed 64-bit</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-js</code>, <code>tramaj-react</code>, <code>tramaj-py</code>, <code>tramaj-go</code></td><td>not yet</td><td>not yet</td><td>every number is a double</td></tr>
</table>

The first three rows are on the `main` branch and in no release yet. Until the
other implementations follow, the same template can give different output on
two of them: `str(1.0)` is `1.0` on the first three rows and `1` on the last.
The corpus cases that depend on the split or on arithmetic are marked with
`"requires"`, and an implementation that does not declare the name skips
them. An existing host moving to the split should read
[Migrating to integers and floats](/migrating-to-integers-and-floats.html).

## PureScript — import as a library, fold with `tramaj-halogen`

`tramaj-purs`, `tramaj-halogen`, `tramaj-cli`, and `playground` are members of one
[Spago](https://github.com/purescript/spago) workspace rooted at this
repository. A new PureScript package placed as a subdirectory of the
workspace, with `tramaj-purs` (and `tramaj-halogen` if you're rendering through
Halogen) listed in its `spago.yaml` dependencies, is picked up by Spago's
workspace discovery with no registry publishing step needed.

The core API:

```
-- Tramaj.Parser
parseProgram :: String -> Either ParseError Program

-- Tramaj.Eval
runProgram     :: Mode    -> LibraryTable -> Json -> Program -> Either EvalError Json
runProgramWith :: Options -> LibraryTable -> Json -> Program -> Either EvalError Json

type Options = { mode :: Mode, arithmetic :: Boolean }
defaultOptions :: Options   -- { mode: Concrete, arithmetic: false }

-- Tramaj.Json
jsonParser :: String -> Either String Json
stringify  :: Json -> String
```

`Mode` is `Concrete` or `Symbolic` ([`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html));
`LibraryTable` supplies whatever the template's `import(...)` calls need.
`Json` is `Tramaj.Json.Json`, not Argonaut's: a JSON value whose numbers keep
their type. Read the context with `jsonParser` and write the result with
`stringify`; `JSON.parse` and `JSON.stringify` cannot tell `3` from `3.0`.
`runProgram` runs without the arithmetic profile;
`runProgramWith (defaultOptions { arithmetic = true })` turns it on.
`Tramaj.Analysis.deepArithmeticOps` reports which arithmetic names a program
and its libraries reference, so a host without the profile can refuse one
before running it.
`runProgram` gives you the evaluated document as JSON directly, already
encoded per [`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html). If you need the
`Node` value itself rather than its JSON encoding — to fold it without a
round-trip through JSON — `evalProgramWithEmissions` is the lower-level
entry point that stops one step earlier.

If your host renders through [Halogen](https://github.com/purescript-halogen/purescript-halogen),
don't write a fold at all — `tramaj-halogen` already has one:

```
-- Tramaj.Halogen
foldToHalogen
  :: forall action slots m
   . (String -> String -> Json -> Maybe action)
  -> Node
  -> Array (ComponentHTML action slots m)
```

The first argument is a `dispatch` function: event type, action key, and
JSON payload in, an optional host action out. A read-only host supplies
`\_ _ _ -> Nothing`. The result is an `Array`, not a single element, because
a fragment contributes several siblings with no wrapper element. Call
`validateAttrNames` on the `Node` first — attribute names aren't validated
by the fold itself.

[`playground/src/Playground/Main.purs`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/playground/src/Playground/Main.purs)
is the canonical worked example of the whole pipeline —
`Tramaj.Parser` → `Tramaj.Eval` → `Tramaj.Halogen.foldToHalogen` — including
`dispatchAction`, which turns `action(...)` clicks into real Halogen actions
for an *interactive* host (a read-only host just passes `const Nothing`
instead).

## Haskell — the library exists, the fold doesn't yet

`tramaj-hs` is an independent Haskell port, built on megaparsec and aeson,
that targets the same grammar as the PureScript `tramaj-purs` and shares its
conformance corpus. It's a normal Cabal library exposing `Tramaj.Ast`,
`Tramaj.Parser`, `Tramaj.Eval`, `Tramaj.Node`, `Tramaj.Json`,
`Tramaj.Analysis`, and `Tramaj.Types` — so a Haskell host can depend on it directly (as a
`source-repository-package` or path dependency; it isn't published to
Hackage), the same way a PureScript host depends on `tramaj-purs`.

What it does *not* have yet is a `tramaj-halogen` equivalent — no fold to
Lucid, Blaze, or any other Haskell HTML/UI library ships in this
repository. A Haskell host gets the parser, the evaluator, and the `Node`
JSON encoding (kept in lockstep with the PureScript `Tramaj.Node`); writing
the fold to your actual rendering library is on you. The shape to follow is
`tramaj-halogen`'s: case on `Text`/`Element`/`Fragment`, recurse into
children, and decide what an `action(...)` payload means in your host.

The evaluator takes and returns `Tramaj.Json.Json`, a JSON value with
separate `JInt` and `JFloat` constructors, where it used to take
`Data.Aeson.Value`. `jsonParser` reads JSON text and types each number by
its text; `stringify` writes it back. `fromAeson` and `toAeson` bridge to
aeson, with a loss: aeson has already forgotten whether a number was written
`3` or `3.0`, so `fromAeson` makes every whole number that fits 64 bits an
integer. Integers cover the signed 64-bit range here, wider than the
PureScript implementation's. Arithmetic is off in `runProgram` and on with
`runProgramWith defaultOptions {optArithmetic = True}`.

## Rust — the library exists, plus its own CLI

`tramaj-rs` is an independent, hand-written Rust port of the same grammar
and evaluation semantics as the PureScript `tramaj-purs` and Haskell `tramaj-hs`,
held to the same shared conformance corpus (`corpus/cases/`, including
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html)'s symbols/constraints
and [`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html)'s nominal types, not just the
core language, with the two number types and the arithmetic profile,
see *Numbers at the boundary*). It's a normal Cargo library crate — `tramaj_rs::{ast, parser,
eval, json, node, analysis, types}` — depended on as a path or git dependency (not
published to crates.io yet), the same way a PureScript host depends on
`tramaj-purs` or a Haskell host depends on `tramaj-hs`:

```rust
// tramaj_rs::parser
pub fn parse_program(src: &str) -> Result<Program, ParseError>;

// tramaj_rs::json
pub fn parse(src: &str) -> Result<Json, String>;
pub fn stringify(v: &Json) -> String;

// tramaj_rs::eval
pub struct Options { pub mode: Mode, pub arithmetic: bool }

pub fn run_program(
    mode: Mode,
    libs: &LibraryTable,
    ctx: &Json,
    program: &Program,
) -> Result<Json, EvalError>;

pub fn run_program_with(
    options: &Options,
    libs: &LibraryTable,
    ctx: &Json,
    program: &Program,
) -> Result<Json, EvalError>;
```

`tramaj_rs::json::Json` is the JSON type of this port: like
`serde_json::Value`, with an integer (`Json::Int`, an `i64`) and a float
(`Json::Float`) as two variants. Read the context with `json::parse` and
write the result with `json::stringify`, so that `3` and `3.0` stay two
values both ways. `json::from_serde` and `json::to_serde` bridge to a
`serde_json::Value` a host already holds; their documentation says what each
direction loses. Integers cover the signed 64-bit range, as in the Haskell
implementation: a literal, a context number or a result outside it is
refused, never rounded. Arithmetic is off in `run_program` and on with
`run_program_with(&Options { mode, arithmetic: true }, ...)`;
`analysis::deep_arithmetic_ops` lists the arithmetic names a program and its
libraries reference, for a host that leaves the profile off.

`run_program` gives you the evaluated result as JSON directly, already
encoded per [`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html) when the program
produces a document, or the concrete/symbolic envelope from
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html) §5 otherwise — the same shape
`runProgram` returns on the PureScript and Haskell sides. As with Haskell,
there's no `tramaj-halogen` equivalent shipped yet: a Rust host gets the
parser, evaluator, `Node` JSON codec, and static analysis, and writes its
own fold following the same shape (case on `Text`/`Element`/`Fragment`,
recurse into children, decide what an `action(...)` payload means in your
host).

Unlike the Haskell port, Rust also has a `tramaj-cli` equivalent —
`tramaj-cli-rs` — mirroring `tramaj-cli`'s command surface field-for-field:
the same `evaluate`/`analyze <imports|actions|holes|unsupplied|constraints|symbols|arithmetic|types|card|all>`
subcommands, the same `--lib name=path`/`--mode concrete|symbolic`/`--arithmetic` flags,
and the same JSON output shapes. If you'd rather shell out than link a Rust
library into your host — including from a non-Rust host — `tramaj-cli-rs` is
an alternative to `tramaj-cli` with no Node.js runtime required. Its integer
range is the signed 64-bit one, where `tramaj-cli` has the guaranteed 53 bits.

## JavaScript / TypeScript — import as a library, fold with `tramaj-react`

`tramaj-js` is an independent, hand-written TypeScript port of the same
grammar and evaluation semantics as the PureScript `tramaj-purs`, Haskell
`tramaj-hs`, and Rust `tramaj-rs`, held to the same shared conformance
corpus (`corpus/cases/`, including
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html)'s symbols/constraints and
[`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html)'s nominal types, not just the
core language; the two number types and arithmetic are not in this port
yet). It's a normal npm package with no React or DOM dependency
— a plain Node or browser host can depend on it the same way a PureScript
host depends on `tramaj-purs`:

```typescript
// parseProgram, tryParseProgram
function parseProgram(src: string): Program; // throws ParseError

// runProgram
function runProgram(
  mode: Mode,
  libs: LibraryTable,
  ctx: Json,
  program: Program,
): Json; // throws EvalError
```

`Mode` is `"concrete"` or `"symbolic"` ([`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html));
`runProgram` gives you the evaluated result as JSON directly, already
encoded per [`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html) when the program
produces a document, or the concrete/symbolic envelope from
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html) §5 otherwise — the same shape
`runProgram` returns on the PureScript, Haskell, and Rust sides. `evalProgram`
is the lower-level entry point if you want the `Node` value itself rather
than its JSON encoding, to fold it without a round-trip through JSON.

If your host renders through React, don't write a fold at all —
`tramaj-react` already has one, the same shape as `tramaj-halogen`'s:

```typescript
type Dispatch = (
  event: string,
  key: string,
  payload: Json,
) => MouseEventHandler<Element> | undefined | null;

function foldToReact(dispatch: Dispatch, node: Node): ReactNode[];
```

`dispatch` decides which `action(...)` entries become a real event handler
— it inspects the event type itself (`foldToReact` doesn't), and returns
`undefined`/`null` for ones it wants to ignore. Every action a `dispatch`
call accepts is wired to `onClick`. The result is an array, not a single
element, because a fragment contributes several siblings with no wrapper —
splice it into your own container, `<>{foldToReact(dispatch, node)}</>`.
Call `validateAttrNames` on the `Node` first, same caveat as
`tramaj-halogen`: attribute names aren't validated by the fold itself.

If you'd rather not add a JS dependency at all — or your host isn't Node —
`tramaj-cli` (or `tramaj-cli-rs`) as a subprocess is still the same option
open to any other language; see [Getting started](/howto-humans.html) for
the exact CLI invocations, and *Any other language* below for the general
shape of folding its JSON output by hand.

## Python — import as a library, standard library only

`tramaj-py` is an independent, hand-written Python port of the same grammar
and evaluation semantics as the other implementations, held to the
same shared conformance corpus (`corpus/cases/`, including
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html)'s symbols/constraints and
[`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html)'s nominal types; the two number
types and arithmetic are not in this port yet). It needs
nothing outside the standard library (Python 3.9+), so it drops into a data
pipeline, a notebook or a backend service without a Node or PureScript
toolchain as a runtime dependency. It's a normal package — `tramaj` with
`ast`, `parser`, `evaluator`, `node`, `analysis`, `typesys` and `jsonval`
modules, laid out one-to-one like `tramaj-js` — installed from the
repository's `tramaj-py/` directory (not published to PyPI yet):

```python
from tramaj import parse_program, run_program

program = parse_program('.p($ctx.name)')          # raises ParseError
run_program("concrete", {}, {"name": "web"}, program)  # raises EvalError
```

`run_program` gives you the evaluated result as plain Python JSON values
(`dict`/`list`/`str`/numbers/`bool`/`None`), already encoded per
[`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html) when the program produces a
document, or the concrete/symbolic envelope from
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html) §5 otherwise — the same shape
`runProgram` returns on every other side. A library table is a `dict` from
import name to parsed program; the mode is `"concrete"` or `"symbolic"`.
`eval_program` is the lower-level entry point if you want the `Node` value
itself rather than its JSON encoding. Every number is still a double
in this port: an exact integer comes back as an `int`, and `str` renders as
ECMAScript's `Number::toString` does. That matches the JavaScript and
Go ports byte-for-byte, and differs from the PureScript, Haskell and Rust
implementations for a whole-valued float (`1` here, `1.0` there).

As with Haskell and Rust, no fold is shipped: a Python host writes its own
following the same shape (case on `text`/`element`/`fragment`, recurse into
children, decide what an `action(...)` payload means in your host). Python
also has its own `tramaj-cli` equivalent, `python -m tramaj`, mirroring
`tramaj-cli-rs`'s `evaluate`/`analyze` subcommands, flags and JSON output
shapes, so a non-Python host with a Python interpreter around can shell out
to it instead of to Node.

## Go — import as a module, standard library only

`tramaj-go` is an independent, hand-written Go port of the same grammar and
evaluation semantics as the other implementations, held to the same shared
conformance corpus (`corpus/cases/`, including
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html)'s symbols/constraints and
[`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html)'s nominal types; the two number
types and arithmetic are not in this port yet). It needs nothing
outside the standard library (Go 1.21+), so it compiles into a service or a
static binary with no Node, Python or PureScript toolchain as a runtime
dependency. It's one package, `tramaj`, in the repository's `tramaj-go/`
directory (no tagged module release yet):

```go
import tramaj "github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/tramaj-go"

program, err := tramaj.ParseProgram(`.p($ctx.name)`) // err is a *tramaj.ParseError
ctx, _ := tramaj.ParseJSON([]byte(`{"name": "web"}`))
out, err := tramaj.RunProgram(tramaj.Concrete, nil, ctx, program) // err is a *tramaj.EvalError
fmt.Println(tramaj.PrettyJSON(out))
```

`RunProgram` gives you the evaluated result as a `tramaj.JSON` value (`nil`,
`bool`, `float64`, `string`, `[]tramaj.JSON` or `*tramaj.Object`, an object
that keeps its keys in order), already encoded per
[`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html) when the program produces a
document, or the concrete/symbolic envelope from
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html) §5 otherwise — the same shape
`runProgram` returns on every other side. A library table is a
`tramaj.Libraries`, a map from import name to parsed program; the mode is
`tramaj.Concrete` or `tramaj.Symbolic`. `EvalProgram` is the lower-level entry
point if you want the `*tramaj.Node` itself rather than its JSON encoding.
Every number is still a `float64` in this port, and `str` renders as
ECMAScript's `Number::toString` does. That matches the JavaScript and
Python ports byte-for-byte, and differs from the PureScript, Haskell and Rust
implementations for a whole-valued float (`1` here, `1.0` there).

As with Haskell, Rust and Python, no fold is shipped: a Go host writes its
own following the same shape (switch on the node's `Type`, recurse into
`Children`, decide what an action attribute means in your host). Go also has
its own `tramaj-cli` equivalent, `tramaj-go` (`go build ./cmd/tramaj-go`),
mirroring `tramaj-cli-rs`'s `evaluate`/`analyze` subcommands, flags and JSON
output shapes.

## Any other language

For a host in a language with no PureScript, Haskell, Rust, JavaScript,
Python or Go interop story — Java, C#, whatever — there are two options:

**(a) Full reimplementation.** Port the parser and evaluator to your
language, the way `tramaj-hs` ports the PureScript original. Worth it only
if you need to *evaluate* templates natively (e.g. embedding evaluation in
a process that can't shell out, or needs it to run in-process for
performance). [`reference.md`](/reference-language.html) is the full
grammar and evaluation semantics to implement against, and the conformance
corpus (`corpus/cases/` in the repository — template + context + expected
output triples) is what the six existing implementations are held to; a
new implementation should be checked against the same corpus.

**(b) Partial implementation, starting from `tramaj-cli`'s output.** For
most hosts this is the pragmatic choice: don't reimplement the parser or
evaluator at all. Shell out to `tramaj-cli`:

```bash
tramaj-cli template.tramaj context.json
tramaj-cli --arithmetic template.tramaj context.json   # with the arithmetic builtins
```

which prints the evaluated `Node` as JSON on stdout, exactly per
[`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html). In that JSON `3` is an integer
and `3.0` a float; a host that needs the difference reads it with a parser
that keeps it. Your host only needs to write
the *fold* — parse that JSON, case on whether each node is text, an
element, or a fragment, recurse into children, and decide what each
element's `action(...)` entries mean in your host — the same shape as
`tramaj-halogen`'s fold, just written against decoded JSON instead of a
native `Node` value. `tramaj-cli`'s `analyze` subcommands
(`imports`/`actions`/`holes`/`unsupplied`/`constraints`/`symbols`/`arithmetic`/`types`)
are available the same way, without a context file, if your host wants the
static-analysis facts from [Properties](/properties.html) rather than a
rendered document.

This is also the fastest way to prototype an integration in any language
before deciding whether a full reimplementation is ever worth it.

See the [Roadmap](/roadmap.html) for what a new implementation needs to
cover and where to start if you want to build one; Rust, JavaScript, Python
and Go each have one now (`tramaj-rs`, `tramaj-js`, `tramaj-py` and
`tramaj-go`, above).

=base:main-css.tramaj-json
{ "format": "css"
, "contents":
  [ ""
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/dev.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/colors.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/article.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/navigation.css\";"
  , "@import \"`$ctx.pathPrefix`/css/documentation.css\";"
  ]
}

